-- title:
--   Order-two point gives a degree-one kernel polynomial
-- statement:
--   Let $F$ be a field with decidable equality, let $W$ be a Weierstrass curve over $F$ which is elliptic (nonzero discriminant, via the `IsElliptic` instance), and let $Q$ be a point of the associated affine curve `W.toAffine` whose additive order is exactly $2$. Write $(x_Q,y_Q)$ for `Q.coordsOrZero`, the pair of coordinates of $Q$, defined to be $(0,0)$ at the point at infinity and $(x,y)$ at an affine point `Affine.Point.some x y _`. The conclusion is that the monic linear polynomial $X - x_Q \in F[X]$ satisfies the predicate `W.IsTwoKernel`, which by definition consists of three assertions: its natural degree is at most $1$; its coefficient in degree $1$ equals $1$; and it divides $W.\Psi_2\mathrm{Sq}$, the square of the $2$-division polynomial of $W$. Thus $x_Q$ is a root of $\Psi_2^2$ and $X - x_Q$ is a monic degree-one kernel polynomial cutting out the order-two subgroup generated by $Q$.
--
--   This is the statement that a rational point of exact order $2$ on an elliptic curve yields a $\Gamma_0(2)$-structure presented in kernel-polynomial form, the case $p^k = 2$ of the construction of prime-power $\Gamma_0$-level structures. It is used in the production of cyclic subgroups with prescribed $\Gamma_0$-behaviour over algebraically closed fields and in the corresponding statement for the Tate curve.
